## What the results mean

These are promising, very one-sided results at THIS control. They are insufficient
for an absolute rating or a human/FIDE claim. The reference CCRL 40/15 ratings
identify opponents; our time control, host, runtime, build and hash settings differ.
Do not add a score-derived Elo difference to a published CCRL rating and call that
Mitchell's established rating.

The old unrated Level 35/50 matches and pre-hash-fix speed figures are historical.
They must not be pooled with this repaired-engine run. The earlier faulty campaign
is separately archived under validation/regressions/failed-campaign/.

## Fastest defensible route to a rating estimate

1. Choose the scale first: a local computer-engine rating under stated conditions,
   a CCRL-comparable estimate, and a human FIDE rating are different claims.
2. Run short colour-paired pilots against stronger, independent opponents. Seek
   opponents giving about 35-65% score at the intended final control. The current
   opponents are too one-sided to narrow an upper rating efficiently. Choose
   final opponents before collecting the final rating sample; publish pilot games
   separately instead of selectively mixing favourable pilot results into it.
3. Freeze the engine, versions, openings, control, hash/thread settings, referee,
   runtime and host details. Use the SAME control for the final pool and reference
   matches. For a CCRL-comparable claim, follow the relevant list's published
   control/hardware methodology; one second per move is not CCRL 40/15.
4. Concentrate roughly 200-400 final games against 2-4 competitive opponents,
   with balanced colours, varied openings, and no pondering. Run games in parallel
   only within available CPU and memory limits; never oversubscribe to gain speed.
5. Fit the pool with an established estimator such as Ordo or BayesElo. Publish
   raw PGNs, anchored references and uncertainty. Include opponent calibration
   uncertainty and differences in test conditions, not just game-sampling error.

Around a 50% score, an idealized independent decisive-game model gives about
+/-48 Elo sampling error at 95% for 200 games, or +/-34 for 400. These are planning
figures, NOT this engine's Elo, NOT a guarantee, and NOT an overall confidence
interval: draws, opening pairs, anchors and systematic differences change it.
SPRT can efficiently decide whether a version improved; it does not by itself
establish an absolute Elo rating. A computer pool alone cannot establish FIDE Elo.

## Repeat correctness and evidence checks

From the repository root, with Node.js 24 and Python 3.10+:

    node scripts/test.cjs
    node scripts/test-neural.cjs
    node scripts/test-hash-moves.cjs
    python scripts/test-campaign.py

Coverage includes standard perft, special moves, mate/check evasions,
underpromotion, KPK, short timeouts, all book lines, independent neural-reference
scores, scalar/SIMD agreement, incremental accumulators, the real historical hash
collision, six formerly failing game positions, invalid TT-entry injection,
strict history replay, automatic campaign pause, and archived-result verification.

Recompute the archived counts without installing any external chess engine:

    python scripts/independent-campaign.py validation/independent/results-58/campaign.json --summary-only

Only the generated update timestamp differs from the saved summary. The paired
bootstrap uses complete normal-result colour pairs; it does not include a
forfeit or half of an interrupted pair. Small/degenerate samples have no interval.

Optional browser checks require separately installed Playwright and Chromium:

    node scripts/browser-test.cjs

Set CHROMIUM_PATH if Chromium is not at /usr/bin/chromium. This checks offline
loading, responsive worker search, all 65 levels, 38 lessons, cancellation and MIT
notices. This environment blocks direct file:// navigation, so the test serves
the HTML from memory and then disables network access.

## Repeat the recorded 200-game protocol on another machine

External programs are test-only; no third-party engine, referee or compatibility
library is distributed with the MIT game. Install independently:

- Fastchess 1.8.2-alpha (the recorded build reports f618e34, July 26, 2026).
- Fruit 2.1, built from the source revision in campaign.json using its supplied
  Makefile. Our x86-64 build can differ from the historical CCRL executable.
- MinimalChess 0.6, the official Linux release. Its older .NET runtime may require
  a separately installed compatibility libssl1.1 and invariant-globalization
  setting on modern Linux; resolve runtime setup before collecting games.
- Node.js v24.22.1 for the closest match to the recorded runtime, and Python.

Opponent sources and reference citations are recorded in:

    validation/independent/results-58/campaign.json
    validation/independent-campaign.md

Prepare a NEW, empty output directory with local executable paths:

    python scripts/prepare-benchmark.py \
      --out /tmp/mitchell-repeat \
      --fastchess /absolute/path/to/fastchess \
      --fruit /absolute/path/to/fruit \
      --minimal /absolute/path/to/MinimalChessEngine \
      --node /absolute/path/to/node \
      --revision bdb5873406a2b3336523f3a19f16c4a1bc9ebd46

This extracts the tested HTML, UCI adapter, loader and opening file from Git,
freezes them in the new directory, hashes the supplied binaries and records local
runtime versions. It refuses to overwrite an existing run. Environment variables
are inherited from your terminal but are not copied into the manifest; record any
explicit compatibility-library setup you use when publishing your own results.
The runner verifies the frozen engine, adapter, openings and opponent hashes.
Time-limited play need not produce identical moves or outcomes on another machine;
this reproduces the protocol and inputs, not a predetermined score.

Then run the command printed by the preparer, normally:

    python scripts/independent-campaign.py /tmp/mitchell-repeat/campaign.json

Protocol: 100 games per opponent, paired colours across the same 50 openings,
1 second per move, equal 250 ms scheduling grace, no books or pondering, no
score-based resignation/draw adjudication, and no move-limit draws. Fruit and
MinimalChess use 32 MB hash; Mitchell retains approximately 36 MB. Two games
run concurrently, in twenty-game batches. Actual move times remain in PGN;
equal time limits do not imply equal time actually used. A full run takes hours.

The runner saves PGNs, logs, configuration, per-game checkpoints, status.json and
summary.json. It pauses on abnormal termination. For an ordinary interruption,
with unchanged frozen inputs and no unresolved fault:

    python scripts/independent-campaign.py /tmp/mitchell-repeat/campaign.json --resume

An unresolved recorded forfeit will trigger the safety pause again. Do not edit
or delete games merely to bypass it. Review the cause and publish a new protocol
if changing an adapter, opponent, adjudication or clock policy. In particular,
normalizing MinimalChess's uppercase promotion output would be a separately
labelled adapter experiment, not an unmodified repeat of the recorded one.

## Keep result sets separate

- Archived 58-game snapshot: repaired hash, independent Fastchess referee,
  incomplete and paused on the opponent's UCI-format fault.
- Original 12-game failed campaign: pre-fix hash defect and six Mitchell forfeits.
- Fourteen clean referee regression/preflight games: deliberately selected checks,
  not a rating sample; see validation/hash-move-fix.md.
- Original self-play and pre-fix speed runs: historical development evidence only.

When publishing new results, include the exact revision and HTML hash, complete
PGNs, counts of abnormal/incomplete games, full configuration and an honest
statement of which rating scale, if any, the evidence supports.
